// spec: a jump into the scope of a variable past its initialization is an error, as C++'s [stmt.dcl] 3 makes it ill-formed (clang: "cannot jump from this goto statement to its label", "jump bypasses variable initialization"): a goto forward into a block past an object's construction, a goto backward into a block already left; a case label after a declaration in the switch's body is none, each case being a scope of its own in kpp (design.md "No implicit fallthrough" rule 4: kpp/no_implicit_fallthrough)
// found by thread B (2026-11-05), comparing kpp with clang: kpp ran the jumps and destroyed, at the scope's end, an object it never built (`inside -1` with no `+1`)
// error: jump_past_initialization_is_error.kpp:17: error: the goto to inside skips the initialization of a variable in scope at the label
// error: jump_past_initialization_is_error.kpp:33: error: the goto to back enters the scope of a variable past its initialization
// (a case label after a declaration was an error here until no implicit fallthrough became kpp's default: each case
// is a scope of its own now, as kpp/no_implicit_fallthrough's scoped() shows)
class Noisy
{
public:
  int id;
  Noisy(int i) : id(i) { std::println("+", this.id); }
  ~Noisy() { std::println("-", this.id); }
};
int forward(int x)
{
  if (x)
    goto inside;
  {
    Noisy n(1);
  inside:
    std::println("inside");
  }
  return 0;
}
int backward(int x)
{
  {
    int k = 5;
  back:
    x += k;
  }
  if (x < 20)
    goto back;
  return x;
}
int main()
{
  return forward(1) + backward(1);
}
